There are smooth exponentially localized $Q\mathcal K$-valued angular coefficients $T_2,T_3,T_4$, bounded uniformly for small $\lambda$, such that $$QF=bG+\lambda A
       \left(\frac{T_2}{r^2}+\frac{T_3}{r^3}+\frac{T_4}{r^4}\right)
       +\mathcal E_Q,\qquad
 \|\partial_x^\nu\mathcal E_Q\|
       \leq C_D\lambda Ar^{-5}\quad(|\nu|\leq2).
 \label{eq:complementary-expansion}$$ Writing $Y_z=y_{1z}+y_{2z}$, one has $$\begin{split}
 T_2&=-a_0B_Q^{-1}QW_2\phi,\\
 T_2|_{\omega_z=0}&=T_3|_{\omega_z=0}=0,\\
 T_4|_{\omega_z=0}
   &=\frac12B_Q^{-1}\Delta_{\mathbb S^2}T_2|_{\omega_z=0}
     =-a_0'(0)B_Q^{-2}QY_z\phi .
 \end{split}
 \label{eq:equatorial-coefficient}$$ The last vector has norm bounded below by a positive constant at fixed $D$, uniformly for sufficiently small $\lambda$. All $T_j$ have distinguished spin up.

Suppose $D$ satisfies [prop:two-centre-spectrum](#prop:two-centre-spectrum) and $$k_0>\sqrt{0.98},\qquad \gamma<\sqrt{49/45}.$$ Fix $t\in(0,1]$. Assume the two-electron ground level is isolated and simple with an even ionic singlet, and the three-electron ground level in $S_z=1/2$ is isolated and simple, is an absolute ground level, and has a real axisymmetric odd ground state. Assume $n_t>0$, and set $$k=\sqrt{2(E_2(t)-E_3(t))},\qquad
 A_t(r)=e^{-kr}r^{(2-2t)/k-1}.$$ Suppose $$\begin{gathered}
 2\epsilon_b-E_3(t)>0.49,\qquad 0.99<k<\gamma,\\
 \inf_{\omega_z>0}\liminf_{r\to\infty}
 \frac{\|P_tF_{\mathrm{true},t}(r\omega)\|}
      {\omega_z A_t(r)}>0.
 \end{gathered}
 \label{eq:fixed-channel-hypotheses}$$ Then $n_t$ has the local regularity in [lem:density-regularity](#lem:density-regularity), is smooth away from the nuclei, and satisfies [eq:plane-tail](#eq:plane-tail), [eq:plane-translate](#eq:plane-translate), [eq:bulk-translate](#eq:bulk-translate) at this fixed coupling, with $A=A_t$ and suitable positive constants. Membership of $t$ in the preceding perturbative interval is not required.

Let $n$ be a positive, continuous, even density with $\int n=3$, $n\in H^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbf R^3)$, and $\sqrt n\in H^1(\mathbf R^3)$. The problem $$\label{eq:angle-minimum}
 \begin{aligned}
 &\min_{\vartheta\in H^1(n;\mathbf R)}
       \int_{\mathbf R^3}n|\nabla\vartheta|^2,\\
 &\text{subject to }\quad
       \vartheta\text{ odd},\qquad
       \int_{\mathbf R^3}n\sin^2\vartheta=1
 \end{aligned}$$ has a minimizer $\theta$ satisfying $0\le\theta\le\pi/2$ on $\Omega_+$. Every such folded minimizer has a multiplier $d>0$ and satisfies $$\label{eq:angle-euler}
 L_n\theta:=n^{-1}\operatorname{div}(n\nabla\theta)
       =-d\sin(2\theta)
 \quad\hbox{on }\mathbf R^3.$$ It belongs locally to $W^{2,p}$ for every $p<6$, and $$0<\theta<\pi/2\quad\hbox{on }\Omega_+.$$ In particular, it is locally $C^{1,\alpha}$ for every $0<\alpha<1/2$. Axial symmetry of $n$ is not required for this proposition, and axial symmetry of $\theta$ is not asserted.

For a folded minimizer define $$\label{eq:angle-orbitals}
 g=\sqrt n\cos\theta,\qquad h=\sqrt n\sin\theta.$$ Then $g,h\in H^1(\mathbf R^3)\cap H^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbf R^3)$, $g$ is even and strictly positive, and $h$ is odd and strictly positive on $\Omega_+$. Moreover, $$\|g\|_2^2=2,\qquad \|h\|_2^2=1,\qquad
 \langle g,h\rangle=0,\qquad g^2+h^2=n.$$ The real function $$\label{eq:formal-potential}
 V=\frac{\Delta g}{2g}
   =\frac{\Delta\sqrt n}{2\sqrt n}
        -\frac{|\nabla\theta|^2}{2}+d\sin^2\theta$$ belongs to $L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbf R^3)$, and $$\label{eq:orbital-equations}
 (-\Delta/2+V)g=0,\qquad
 (-\Delta/2+V)h=dh$$ in distributions. On the median plane, $g=\sqrt n$ and $h=0$.

Suppose, for $p\ge P_0$ and $0\le z\le L$, that $m(p,z)=e^{2kp}n(p,z)>0$ satisfies $$\label{eq:strip-comparison-data}
 \begin{aligned}
 |m_p|+|m_{pp}|&\le\eta m,\qquad 0<\eta\le1,\\
 \frac{m(p,t)}{m(p,s)}&\le C
       \qquad(0\le t\le s\le L).
 \end{aligned}$$ Let $U(p,\cdot)$ solve $$\label{eq:strip-ode}
 n^{-1}(nU_z)_z=\mu^2U,\qquad U(p,0)=1,\quad U(p,L)=0.$$ It is positive and decreasing on $0\le z<L$, and $$\label{eq:strip-horizontal-derivatives}
 |U_p|+|U_{pp}|\le C_2\eta U,$$ where $C_2$ depends only on $C,L,\mu$. When $C\le100$, one may take $C_2<10^{30}$.

For sufficiently large $D$, there are $\lambda_0>0$ and $R_0<\infty$ such that every folded minimizing angle for $0\le\lambda<\lambda_0$ satisfies $$\label{eq:exterior-weighted-gap}
 \frac12\int_{\Omega_+}g^2|\nabla\xi|^2
       \ge0.03\int_{\Omega_+}g^2|\xi|^2$$ for tests supported in $\{|x|>R_0\}$. No zero trace on $z=0$ is required. The inequality holds throughout the corresponding weighted form domain.

For the physical ground-state density $n=n_\Psi$ of [thm:main](#thm:main), $\partial_{\mathcal V}T_{\mathrm{ens}}[n]=\varnothing$. Moreover, there is no $w\in\mathcal V$ such that, for every $m\in\mathcal D$, $$\lim_{t\downarrow0}
 \frac{E_{\mathrm{Hxc}}[n+t(m-n)]-E_{\mathrm{Hxc}}[n]}{t}
 =\int_{\mathbf R^3}w(m-n).
 \label{eq:hxc-full-segment-variation}$$
